WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.542
The book quotes the mandates rather
than inflating them, and so will I.

2
00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:09.980
Read together, they ask for agency-controlled
resolvers that log, encrypt, validate,

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.920
send internet-bound queries
through CISA's Protective DNS,

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.575
and can't be routed around.

5
00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:21.287
The slide lists each source,
from OMB M-22-09 to the logging

6
00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:26.212
baseline in M-26-14 and the
long-standing DNSSEC requirement.

7
00:00:26.212 --> 00:00:29.451
One point on FedRAMP: it
certifies a cloud service,

8
00:00:29.451 --> 00:00:33.874
not an architecture, and the agency
still authorizes its own system.

9
00:00:33.874 --> 00:00:36.675
A Moderate certification
doesn't cover High,

10
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:40.687
and on-premises appliances sit
inside the agency's own boundary.

11
00:00:40.687 --> 00:00:46.894
Every chapter follows one sequence:
inventory, coexist, migrate authority,

12
00:00:46.894 --> 00:00:53.100
federate the clouds, decommission AD
DNS and DHCP, then harden and evidence.

13
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.580
Five decisions carry most of the risk,
and we'll meet each one along the way.
